.TH CWKEYER 1 "2026" "ham-cwkeyer" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cwkeyer \- low-latency iambic CW sidetone keyer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cwkeyer
.I /dev/input/eventX
.RI [ wpm ]
.RI [ freq_hz ]
.RI [ dit_key ]
.RI [ dah_key ]
.RI [ nograb ]
.RI [ decode ]
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-help
.br
.B cwkeyer
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cwkeyer
reads an evdev keyboard-emulating paddle adapter and generates an ALSA
sidetone. It implements the Curtis 8044 iambic keyer, with selectable mode
A or B and dot/dash paddle memory, and then decodes to text.
.PP
The paddle inputs are debounced the way the 8044's RC input filter does it:
a level change is accepted only after it has been stable for the debounce
hold time.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I /dev/input/eventX
The evdev device to read. Use
.BR evtest (8)
or
.BR libinput (1)
to find it. A normal keyboard is usually
.IR /dev/input/event3 .
.TP
.I wpm
Keying speed in words per minute (default 12, range 1\(en300).
.TP
.I freq_hz
Sidetone frequency in hertz (default 800).
.TP
.IR dit_key ", " dah_key
evdev key names or codes for the dit and dah paddles (default
.B LEFTCTRL
and
.BR RIGHTCTRL ).
Known names include
.BR COMMA ,
.BR DOT ,
.BR LEFT ,
.BR RIGHT ,
.BR Z ,
and
.BR X ;
a
.B KEY_
prefix and raw numeric codes are also accepted.
.TP
.B nograb
Do not take exclusive control of the input device; useful when testing on
the keyboard you are typing on.
.TP
.B decode
Print each keyed symbol decoded to text, without newlines. Unknown symbols
are ignored, and a pause longer than seven dits prints a space.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
ALSA PCM to open (default
.BR pulse ).
.TP
.B CW_ALSA_LATENCY_US
Requested ALSA latency in microseconds (default 20000). ALSA may negotiate
a different buffer and period; the achieved values are printed at startup.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_MODE
Curtis iambic mode,
.B a
or
.B b
(case-insensitive).
.B b
is the default. In mode B a released squeeze leaves the opposite memory set
and one extra element is keyed; in mode A it stops at the element in
progress.
.TP
.B CW_KEYER_DEBOUNCE_MS
Paddle debounce hold time in milliseconds, 0\(en50 (default 5). Any level
change shorter than this is treated as contact bounce and discarded. Set to
0 to disable debouncing.
.TP
.B PULSE_SINK
PulseAudio/PipeWire sink for the sidetone. If
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
is
.B pulse
and this is unset or empty, it defaults to
.BR cw_out ,
the combined sink created by
.BR cw-mic-mix (1).
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
stop the keyer and restore the terminal and the input grab.
.B SIGHUP
and
.B SIGQUIT
do the same.
.B SIGTSTP
(Ctrl+Z) releases the terminal and the input device before suspending and
re-establishes them on
.BR SIGCONT .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3
12 WPM, 800 Hz on a keyboard.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3 25 700
25 WPM at 700 Hz.
.TP
.B cwkeyer /dev/input/event3 25 700 COMMA DOT nograb decode
Remap the paddles, do not grab the device, and decode to text.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/input/eventX
Paddle input device.
.TP
.I /dev/input/
evdev devices; see the README for the recommended udev rule.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evtest (8),
.BR libinput (1),
.BR pactl (1)
.PP
The project README and
.B cw-mic-mix
helper.
.SH LICENSE
GPL-3.0-or-later.
